--- a/docs/MANUAL.md
+++ b/docs/MANUAL.md
@@ -1239,6 +1239,14 @@ The script must print a single line (`\n`-terminated) to its output and exit. Th
 * `accountName`, a string containing the normalized account name (in the case of passphrase-based authentication, it is permissible to return the empty string or omit the value)
 * `error`, containing a human-readable description of the authentication error to be logged if applicable
 
+On success, the script may also grant the account operator status and a vhost (for example, when the external system also knows who the network's staff are). These keys are optional; a script that omits them changes nothing:
+
+* `operClass`, the name of an entry in `oper-classes`. The client becomes an IRC operator of that class as soon as it completes registration (or immediately, for a login after registration, e.g. `NS IDENTIFY`). The empty string means "not an operator". There is no oper name or password: the operator status is bound to the login. If the class doesn't exist, the login still succeeds and an error is logged. On rehash, such operators get their class's new definition, and lose operator status if the class has been removed. Operators who used `/OPER` are never affected.
+* `whoisLine`, a string overriding the class's `whois-line` for this operator
+* `vhost`, a string that is stored as the account's vhost (as with `HS SET`; it must pass `accounts.vhosts.valid-regexp` and `max-length`, and `accounts.vhosts.enabled` must be true). The empty string removes the account's vhost. The datastore is only written when the vhost changes, and a user's `HS OFF` is respected.
+
+The same grants can be applied at any time, including to clients that are already connected, with the `/v1/account_grants` API endpoint.
+
